          "transfer": "<p>Choose the matching route. Write one task and one check before opening the example. There can be more than one useful answer.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Fresh source</th><th>Your task</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>L1-T-X</code></td><td>An upload form should accept PDFs up to 2 MB and explain rejected input. No tests have been run.</td><td>Choose a useful testing task that does not claim a result.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>",
          "comparison": "<details><summary>Compare your approach</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Technical:</strong> “Propose a case for a 3 MB PDF. Check the expected rejection against the size rule; record the test as not run.” The requirement supports an expectation, not an observed failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your answer uses a different output, keep it when the source supports it and the check can reveal a mistake. If you reused “make it good,” replace that phrase with a check of a number, claim or missing fact.</p>\n</details>",
          "own": "<p>Use invented details, or reuse a source above. Keep this private while practising.</p>\n<blockquote><p>My source: …</p>\n<p>My one draft task: …</p>\n<p>I will check … against …</p>\n<p>The missing detail I will leave open is …</p></blockquote>\n<p><strong>Self-check:</strong> Can you point to a source? Is the requested result one manageable draft? Could your check catch a specific mistake? Is a missing detail still visible? If one answer is no, revise that line. A long answer is not automatically a good answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Take forward:</strong> keep these four lines for <a href=\"#lesson=2\">Lesson 2: Write a useful request</a>. You will turn them into instructions someone else can follow.</p>"
