          "example": "<p>A good draft can still include information its next reader does not need. Before moving it into a tool or sharing it, make two decisions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Input:</strong> what information does this task need, and may it be used in this place?</li>\n<li><strong>Output:</strong> who should see the result, and for what purpose?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Making a smaller sample can help you keep working. Removing a name, however, does not automatically give you permission to upload the rest. A distinctive quote, a private link or a credential may still be present. For this practice, use the invented sources provided here. The permission boundaries are part of each fictional case, not a new rule for your real workplace.</p>\n<h4>Work — preserve the problem, replace the private material</h4>\n<p><strong>Source W4:</strong> An internal note contains a client&#x27;s name and a private complaint. Its useful process facts are: duplicate acknowledgments were reported; a team member will collect three examples by Thursday. The note is restricted to the internal team. Outside-tool use and publication are not agreed.</p>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> practise turning notes into an action table.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked sample:</strong> “In an invented library team, Helper A will collect two examples of confusing shelf labels by Monday.”</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> use this newly written fictional case for practice. It retains the task/owner/date structure while changing the situation and its details. It is a new practice problem, not a faithful summary of the private note. Keep the original client&#x27;s identity, quotation and private links out; simply changing a name in an otherwise copied complaint would not establish permission to use it elsewhere.</p>",
